@misc{allen2020couch,
    author = "Allen, Amy",
    title = "Critique on the Couch",
    year = "2020",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that critical theory needs psychoanalysis, in the Freudian tradition read through Klein and object relations, to correct what Allen identifies as normative idealism, developmentalism, utopianism and rationalism in the Frankfurt tradition (as listed in a review quoted in the S2 abstract). RELEVANCE: Extends the End of Progress argument: the developmental reading of history is tied to a developmental psychology of the subject, so the source of standards problem is addressed by rethinking the subject of critique rather than by external norms. Allen's reconstructive-versus-genealogical contrast connects directly to the transformative versus reconstructive axis. POSITION: Decolonial/psychoanalytic revision of critical theory; non-developmental, non-teleological conception of critique.",
    keywords = "Allen, psychoanalysis, progress, developmentalism, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "The title of this book suggests a psychoanalysis of critique, as if ‘critical theory needs psychoanalysis’ in the sense that it needs to be psychoanalyzed. Certainly, the book carefully evaluates critical theory; Allen finds, in particular, that the following ‘problems ... have arisen within critical theory as an intellectual project: normative idealism, developmentalism, utopianism, and rationalism’ (p. 187). But these are, of course, not psychoanalytic assessments. The one moment when critique does seem to be on the couch is in the book’s conclusion, which identifies a ‘tendency’ among some contemporary critical theorists to indulge in ‘the seductive comforts and satisfactions of pathologizing those on the other side’ (p. 192). Yet even here, Allen cites just one example of such indulgence, and refrains from probing its deeper motives. After all, the conclusion also counsels readers to ‘resist the temptation to use psychoanalysis as a tool for diagnosing them’ (p. 192). It makes sense that the book does not offer a diagnosis of critical theorists. What the book offers instead are two arguments. The first is that psychoanalysis can provide critical theory with a suitably ‘realistic’ philosophical anthropology and an engaging set of normative aspirations. Such conceptual resources will help critical theorists avoid the problems identified above; they allow critical theory ‘to temper its tendencies toward normative idealism, to rethink its developmental models of self and society, and to theorize the aims and methods of critique beyond utopianism and rationalism’ (p. 24). For this to happen, Allen contends, critical theorists ought to draw less on Freud (or at least on what she calls the ‘official’ Freud) and more on Melanie Klein. Much of the book is devoted to developing Klein’s metapsychology – especially her language of ‘positions’ and her take on drive theory – and its lessons for ethical and political reflection. More precisely, the first four chapters of the book each draw forth one ‘resource,’ or set of resources, from the work of Klein (and, to a lesser extent, from compatible lines of thought in the work of Freud, Lacan, Adorno, and their commentators). Chapter one elaborates Klein’s view of the psyche, which gives us",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

